Looking at this scenario, several considerations carry genuine moral weight simultaneously. The teacher's direct, ground-level knowledge of her students gives her judgment credibility—professionals who observe harm firsthand have some obligation to act on that knowledge rather than blindly comply. At the same time, institutional policies typically exist for reasons of consistency, fairness, and accountability that aren't always visible to any single individual, so there's real value in rule-following even when we disagree. The consequences for students are also highly salient—if the policy genuinely causes harm, that harm matters morally regardless of procedural correctness. Finally, deference to authority carries some weight (institutions often have broader information), but this is the weakest consideration here since she has specific, direct evidence of harm, which somewhat undercuts assumed authority.

Given these overlapping concerns, I don't think any single frame fully captures the situation—professional judgment, harm-avoidance, and rule-of-law concerns all have legitimate claims, even though outcome-focused and judgment-based reasoning seem to carry more weight in a case involving concrete, known harm to specific children.

