This scenario centers on a familiar tension in any tradition-based normative system: the value of methodological continuity versus the need for accurate reasoning when precedent genuinely doesn't fit. The senior scholars have a real point—unchecked reopening of settled matters can fragment shared practice—but "weakens coherence" isn't itself an argument that the analogy is sound. If the situation is truly unprecedented, forcing a bad analogy is a reasoning error, not humility. At the same time, the younger scholar's confidence that the case is unprecedented could itself be mistaken, and only broader deliberation can adjudicate whose reading of "unprecedented" is correct. A consultative process respects both the epistemic authority embedded in tradition and the possibility of genuine novelty, without letting either individual simply assert victory.

For the reasoning question, the deepest issue is not personal loyalty to elders or vindication of the junior scholar, but whether the method matches the reality of the case. Rigid application of precedent where it doesn't fit is a corruption of the tradition's own standards, not a service to them. But the correct process for determining "fit" is communal reasoning, not either party unilaterally declaring the outcome—which favors weighting consultation heavily while still acknowledging the substantive point that stale analogies aren't automatically authoritative.

