The most defensible position seems to be one that honors the *underlying value* (connection to ancestors, continuity of meaning) while recognizing that the *specific form* of fulfilling that value need not be biological reproduction. This avoids both the rigidity of treating the tradition as absolute and the dismissiveness of treating it as merely an imposition with no legitimate claim at all. The couple's own framing—that they can honor ancestors differently—fits this middle path well, though it requires the community and parents to accept some reinterpretation of what "honoring" means.

