For the reasoning, what matters most is how unshared success unravels the warmth that binds the community — undistributed heat curdles into resentment, the classic corrosive social effect this culture fears. The idea that property is simply his own affair (B) sits uneasily with a worldview where hue always implicates the whole spectrum of relations; pure individualism has little purchase here. Dialogue (C) has some appeal as a practical remedy, but it doesn't erase the deeper judgment that a warm community requires warm redistribution.

